CORE msvc-stl
main.cpp
--cpp11 --object-bits 12 --unwindset __std_exception_copy.0:8 --unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ cerr good: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ cerr precision: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ cerr unitbuf: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ cerr tied to cout: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Meaningful only with the MSVC STL (Windows), hence the tag msvc-stl, which
the other platforms exclude (elsewhere the #error makes it fail).
provide_standard_stream_objects did not define the stream objects for the
MSVC STL, and the runtime functions its locale initialization calls had
no model.  Checked with the MSVC 14.51 headers preprocessed by cl
(iostream.ii of the VS 2026 CI job) and --winx64 on Linux: 7 s.
With the MSVC 14.44 STL (VS 2022): --object-bits 12, as there are more than
256 objects, and a bound on the copy loop of __std_exception_copy, whose
message is symbolic on paths that are infeasible but not pruned (symex
did not end; the unwinding assertion holds, so no feasible path is cut).
